Physical premise (IDWT): There is one infinite-dimensional physical manifold. All sectors form one nested chain of submanifolds within it. Particle depth counts ordinary, literal macroscopic spatial dimensions. “Observed” and “unobserved” describe an observer's access to those dimensions, not different kinds of physical dimension. See spatial meaning and mathematical scope. 1. Claim and status The covariant self-contact construction supplies the exact positive eigenvalue D_{N,d} = S(N+1,d) =  inom{N+d}{d} on the occupied coherent mode line of the cumulative mode bundle. This module tests whether that contact eigenvalue can do a second job: select the next occupied mode by resonance before the sector-dependent mass scales are applied. The proposed mechanism is  oxed{ 	ext{projected contact eigenvalue} + 	ext{ground-relative phase} + 	ext{retarded cross-sector source} \longrightarrow 	ext{one resonant mode index}. } It is combined with the exterior-algebra realization of the depth-ten chiral spinor to obtain a candidate gauge-selection block. The status is mixed. Exact mathematics developed here the driven-resonance theorem once the source frequency is specified; the general phase-matching formula for additive, shared-ground, and inclusion--exclusion channels; tensor-product contact eigenvalues; the canonical decomposition \mathbb C^4\otimes\mathbb C^4=\operatorname{Sym}^2\mathbb C^4\oplus \Lambda^2\mathbb C^4 and its 10+3+3 refinement; the projective hyperplane exact sequence producing 35=15+20; the exterior-algebra realization of the chiral Spin(10) spinor; the SU(3)	imes SU(2)	imes U(1) branching and charge table; the integer fixed-point calculation selecting depths six and ten from the critical-spinor closure equation. New physical hypotheses the occupied contact eigenvalue contributes the pre-prism source frequency S(n,d)-1 after removal of the common ground phase; every antisymmetric join contributes one additional phase quantum; tensor-contact eigenvalues act as source frequencies for product-form sites; the charged-lepton projector selects the determinant-singlet 13-dimensional contact carrier; a rank-3+2 polarization split supports the determinant-one gauge subgroup S(U(3)	imes U(2)) at the fibre/representation level. Its global gauge bundle and relation to the geometric connection remain to be specified; this cannot be the full connection on T^{1,0}\mathbb{CP}^5: even its global complex rank-3+2 splitting is obstructed (Section 15.1a). Open calculations derive the ground-subtracted source phase directly from the master action; calculate every cross-sector projector and prove that no unwanted resonant channel survives; establish a topologically admissible global polarization/gauge bundle and derive its rank-3+2 reduction; the literal tangent-bundle split, even without the determinant-one condition, is obstructed; derive the gauge-connection normalizations and coupling constants from the same projected operator. The module therefore gives a sharp route rather than declaring the mode and gauge problems closed. 2. Geometric and operator input Let a particle of real depth d live on the sector manifold Q_d. For a positive mode index n, write N=n-1. The cumulative tangent-mode bundle is \mathcal E_{N,d} = \operatorname{Sym}^{N} \left( \mathbf1_{\mathbb C} \oplus T^*Q_d\otimes\mathbb C ight). Its complex rank is \operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb C}\mathcal E_{N,d} =  inom{N+d}{d} = S(N+1,d). At an occupied projective mode ray [z], the finite-band projection of a point contact is \Lambda_{N,d}([z]) = S(N+1,d) |z;Nangle\langle z;N|. Thus \Lambda_{N,d}([z])|z;Nangle = S(N+1,d)|z;Nangle. The same integer d is the rank of T^*Q_d, the number of independent momenta in the projected principal symbol, and the degree controlling the simplex growth law. Cross-depth interactions act by pullback of the lower-depth field, pushforward of the higher-depth current, and an invariant polarization projector on the shared coordinates. The mode-selection problem is not the existence of candidate eigenvalues. The free sector operator supplies a candidate band for every positive integer n. The problem is to derive why only a finite typed subset is resonantly sourced and remains as an asymptotic particle spectrum. 3. The contact-frequency hypothesis Let P_{[z]}=|z;Nangle\langle z;N| be the occupied coherent-line projector. Define the ground-subtracted contact operator on that line by \widetilde\Lambda_{N,d}([z]) = \Lambda_{N,d}([z])-P_{[z]}. It obeys \widetilde\Lambda_{N,d}([z])|z;Nangle = \left[S(N+1,d)-1ight]|z;Nangle. The subtraction removes the one universal ground occupation already present at N=0. The proposed pre-prism source frequency is therefore  oxed{ \Omega_{n,d}=S(n,d)-1. } This is the central new dynamical hypothesis of the module. It must ultimately follow from the time-dependent projected master equation. The subtraction is not inserted into the rest-mass operator: the physical mass remains m(n,d)=\mu_dS(n,d). It applies only to the ground-relative phase used in the mode-production problem. 4. The resonant firing theorem Let the target pre-prism tower have level operator \widehat N\chi_r=(r-1)\chi_r. Consider a retarded cross-sector source with polarization projector \mathcal I: \left(i\partial_	au-\widehat Night)\phi = h\,\mathcal I J\,e^{-i\Omega	au}. Expand \phi(	au)=\sum_{r\geq1}c_r(	au)\chi_r and write J_r=\langle\chi_r,\mathcal I Jangle. Then i\dot c_r-(r-1)c_r = hJ_r e^{-i\Omega	au}. For c_r(0)=0 and r-1
