t). The three-factor coherent contact has the nonzero eigenvalue  oxed{n_{m top}=72.} This promotes the Euler product from a value identity to an operator candidate: 72 is the eigenvalue of the all-Kähler tensor contact. The remaining physical test is finite and explicit. The invariant projector from Z_{m top} into the d=4 up-type block must have multiplicity one, and the corresponding projectors into unoccupied sectors must vanish. 8. The four-slot contact decomposition and the electron index The four-slot carrier W=\mathbb C\Omega\oplus U, \qquad \dim U=3, has the canonical tensor decomposition W\otimes W = \operatorname{Sym}^2W \oplus \Lambda^2W. Because \Lambda^2W = (\Omega\wedge U) \oplus \Lambda^2U, we obtain  oxed{ W\otimes W = \operatorname{Sym}^2W \oplus (\Omega\wedge U) \oplus \Lambda^2U. } The dimensions are 16=10+3+3. The symmetric piece has \dim\operatorname{Sym}^2W =  inom{4+2-1}{2} =10, which is the first neutrino index. Both remaining pieces have dimension three, but they transform differently under the seed SU(3): \Omega\wedge U\cong U\cong\mathbf3, while \Lambda^2U \cong U^* \cong \overline{\mathbf3}. The determinant contraction U\otimes\Lambda^2U \longrightarrow \Lambda^3U \cong \mathbb C is an SU(3) singlet and is unique up to normalization. This distinguishes \Lambda^2U from the second literal triplet \Omega\wedge U. Define the candidate charged-lepton contact carrier E_e = \operatorname{Sym}^2W \oplus \Lambda^2U. Then  oxed{ \dim E_e=10+3=13. } Equivalently, W\otimes W = E_e \oplus (\Omega\wedge U), so  oxed{16=13+3.} This is the exact carrier identity behind n_s^2=n_e+n_u. It does not say that the electron is composed of a neutrino and an up quark. The spaces above are contact-index carriers. The physical lepton state arises only after the determinant-singlet projector contracts the coloured seed indices. The unresolved statement is that the projected weak/lepton vertex actually selects E_e rather than another 13-dimensional subspace. The decomposition makes that selection canonical once determinant-singlet coupling is imposed. 9. Pascal closure as a projective exact sequence The muon identity 35=15+20 is the rank statement of a standard hyperplane restriction sequence. Let \mathbb P^3\hookrightarrow\mathbb P^4 be a hyperplane defined by a linear form \ell. Then 0 \longrightarrow \mathcal O_{\mathbb P^4}(2) \xrightarrow{	imes\ell} \mathcal O_{\mathbb P^4}(3) \longrightarrow \mathcal O_{\mathbb P^3}(3) \longrightarrow0. The relevant higher cohomology vanishes, so global sections give 0 \longrightarrow H^0(\mathbb P^4,\mathcal O(2)) \longrightarrow H^0(\mathbb P^4,\mathcal O(3)) \longrightarrow H^0(\mathbb P^3,\mathcal O(3)) \longrightarrow0. Their dimensions are \dim H^0(\mathbb P^4,\mathcal O(2)) =  inom{6}{4} =15, \dim H^0(\mathbb P^4,\mathcal O(3)) =  inom{7}{4} =35, and \dim H^0(\mathbb P^3,\mathcal O(3)) =  inom{6}{3} =20. Therefore  oxed{35=15+20} is the dimension identity of an exact sequence rather than an isolated Pascal coincidence. In the tower notation,  oxed{n_\mu=n_{
