abla^{m pol}) \subseteq U(3)	imes U(2). } This removes off-diagonal colour--weak connection components. It does not impose determinant one: that would additionally require the determinant connection to have trivial holonomy. Split preservation and determinant-one reduction are distinct conditions. 15.1 Global tangent-bundle obstruction If the polarization carrier is literally E=T^{1,0}\mathbb{CP}^5, the determinant-one reduction proposed in the earlier version is impossible. The canonical line already identified in Section 11 gives \det E=K_{\mathbb{CP}^5}^{-1}=\mathcal O(6), \qquad c_1(E)=6H
e0, \qquad H=c_1(\mathcal O(1)). Holonomy contained in S(U(3)	imes U(2))\subset SU(5) would induce trivial holonomy on \det E, hence a global parallel determinant section. This contradicts c_1(E)=6H. Equivalently, traceless curvature would give zero real first Chern class. The Euler sequence gives the same result independently: c(T^{1,0}\mathbb{CP}^5)=(1+H)^6. See the Euler-sequence calculation. The pointwise exterior-algebra branching and charge table remain valid. They do not establish this impossible global reduction. A viable completion must specify a separate determinant-trivial gauge bundle, or rigorously distinguish the gauge component from a full geometric connection retaining its central curvature. Neither route is derived from the projected action here; the auxiliary construction below supplies only admissible bundle data. Simply subtracting a local trace does not by itself define a global SU(5) bundle. The existing spinor twist K^{1/2}=\mathcal O(-3) is not a trivialization of \det E. 15.1a The rank-three plus rank-two tangent split is itself obstructed The failure is stronger than the nonzero determinant. Suppose T^{1,0}\mathbb{CP}^5=A_3\oplus B_2 as complex bundles. The Whitney formula and the Euler sequence require an exact degree-five polynomial factorization: c(A_3)c(B_2) =1+6H+15H^2+20H^3+15H^4+6H^5 =(1+2H)(1+H+H^2)(1+3H+3H^2). The two quadratics are irreducible over the rationals. The only possible rank-two factor therefore has (c_1,c_2)=(H,H^2) or (3H,3H^2). Restriction to a linear \mathbb{CP}^3 gives a contradiction: a complex rank-two bundle there with c_1=aH, c_2=bH^2 requires ab even. Both candidates have ab odd. See Opie, Theorem 1.2 and Remark 1.3. Thus no global U(3)	imes U(2) reduction exists on this literal tangent bundle, regardless of the chosen unitary connection. Retaining the central geometric curvature alone cannot repair the split. A line twist also cannot repair it, since any splitting of E\otimes L would untwist to a splitting of E. The pointwise branching remains valid. The splitting audit includes an independent K-theory integrality check. A separate auxiliary gauge-carrier construction realizes the same charge table globally on \mathcal O(-2)^{\oplus3}\oplus\mathcal O(3)^{\oplus2}, but it is additional bundle data, not an identification with the tangent spinor bundle or a derivation of particle zero modes. It is a candidate, not a selected V2 vacuum. 15.2 Remaining source and curvature test Once an admissible bundle is specified, its reduction must be selected by the projected background, not only because it reproduces the desired gauge group. The decisive calculation is the curvature decomposition F^{m pol} =  egin{pmatrix} F_c & F_{cw}\ F_{wc} & F_w \end{pmatrix} and the proof that  oxed{F_{cw}=F_{wc}=0} on the physical vacuum and all allowed perturbative sectors. Vanishing mixed curvature alone is not generally equivalent to a chosen parallel splitting; the connection-level conditions above and global holonomy must also be checked. 16. The critical-spinor fixed points The Gegenbauer criticality condition used in the sector endpoint analysis can be written 4k=(d-2)^2. For even depth, define r= rac d2-1. Then k=r^2. The complex dimension of a chiral spinor in real dimension d is \dim_{\mathbb C}S_d^+ = 2^{d/2-1} = 2^r. Impose the additional critical-spinor closure condition  oxed{k=\dim_{\mathbb C}S_d^+.} It becomes r^2=2^r. The positive integral solutions are r=2, \qquad r=4. Indeed, r=1 and r=3 fail, while for r\geq5 the ratio  rac{2^r}{r^2} is already greater than one and increases monotonically because  rac{2^{r+1}/(r+1)^2}{2^r/r^2} =  rac{2r^2}{(r+1)^2} >1 \qquad(r\geq3). Therefore  oxed{ (d,k)=(6,4), \qquad (d,k)=(10,16). } These values coincide with \dim S_6^+=4=n_s and \dim S_{10}^+=16=k_0=n_s^2. Thus depths six and ten are the only even depths at which Gegenbauer criticality closes on the chiral-spinor dimension. This supplies an exact structural relation between the electron and tau sectors once the critical-spinor equality is accepted as the closure criterion. 17. Unified selection statement The proposed positive mode-selection theorem can be stated as follows. Let J be a source monomial produced by the projected master interaction. Let \mathcal I_J be its invariant polarization projector, and let \Omega_J be its ground-relative contact phase. A target pair (r,d) fires only if  oxed{ \mathcal I_J
eq0, \qquad r-1=\Omega_J, \qquad (r,d)	ext{ lies on an allowed deposit channel}. } The three conditions perform different jobs: the deposit ring fixes the finite topological cell in which a mode may occur; the polarization projector fixes which interaction can populate that cell; contact-phase resonance fixes the integer mode index inside the cell. The existing parity and radiative-width mechanisms then test whether the resulting resonance survives as an asymptotic particle rather than a transient or dressing mode. This separates positive generation from negative exclusion:  oxed{ 	ext{topology supplies the cells}, \quad 	ext{representation theory supplies the channels}, \quad 	ext{contact resonance supplies the indices}, \quad 	ext{stability analysis removes the complement}. } 18. What the construction derives without masses Conditional on the contact-frequency and join-phase hypotheses, the following indices arise without using particle masses: 10, 15, 20, 35 from simplex contact eigenvalues; 13 from the determinant-selected 10+3 carrier inside the four-slot tensor contact; 22 from a shared-seed pushout; 23 from one antisymmetric ground join; 16, 17 from the two-factor tensor contact and its adjacent insertion; 72 from the three-Kähler tensor contact; and 76, 81, 95 from shared-ground and joined-source phase matching. On the coupling side, the depth-ten chiral spinor supplies S_{10}^+ = \Lambda^{m even}\mathbb C^5, and the pointwise rank-3+2 split, with a separate determinant-one condition, supplies the candidate subgroup S(U(3)	imes U(2)), with the complete quark/lepton representation pattern and hypercharges at the fibre level. A globally admissible realization is still required by Section 15. No numerical particle masses enter either construction. 19. Falsifiable operator tests The route fails if any of the following occurs. The time-dependent projected contact term does not generate the ground-relative source phase S(n,d)-1. The invariant projectors admit additional resonant targets outside the committed mode set. Tensor contacts do not enter the source operator multiplicatively. The charged-lepton projector does not select the determinant-singlet 13-carrier. The specified polarization/gauge bundle does not admit the required global reduction. The literal determinant-one reduction of T^{1,0}\mathbb{CP}^5 already fails this test (Section 15). Off-diagonal colour--weak curvature components survive in the physical background. The projected weak generators fail to connect the designated depth pairs with universal normalization. These are binary structural tests rather than parameters that can be retuned against the mass data. 20. Decisive master-action calculation The mode part of the master calculation must produce, after projection and linearization about the condensate,  oxed{ \left(i\partial_	au-\widehat Night)\phi_d = \sum_J h_J\mathcal I_J \exp(-i\Omega_J	au) + 	ext{nonresonant terms}, } with \Omega_J = \sum_i(n_i-1) - \sum_j(m_j-1) + q_J and with every single-parent contact source obeying \Omega_{n,d}=S(n,d)-1. The gauge part must produce  oxed{ P_{10}D_\infty P_{10} = \slashed D_{\mathbb{CP}^5} + \gamma^\mu \left( G_\mu^aT_c^a + W_\mu^rT_w^r + B_\mu Y ight) + \Phi_{10}, } with \operatorname{Hol}(
abla^{m gauge}) \subseteq S(U(3)	imes U(2)), on a separately specified admissible gauge bundle, not as the full holonomy of the tangent bundle, and cross-depth blocks whose exterior-algebra intertwiners reproduce the weak doublet transitions while forbidden colour and Abelian channels vanish. A successful derivation would join mode selection and gauge selection in one projected operator:  oxed{ 	ext{symmetric-power contact} \otimes 	ext{exterior-algebra polarization}. } The symmetric factor determines the resonant integer and mass multiplier. The exterior factor determines charge, colour, weak chirality, and whether the vertex is present. 21. Result The completed proposal is  oxed{  egin{aligned} &	ext{finite-band locality} \Longrightarrow \Lambda_{N,d}=S(N+1,d)P_{[z]},\ &	ext{ground subtraction} \Longrightarrow \Omega_{n,d}=S(n,d)-1,\ &	ext{retarded resonance} \Longrightarrow n_{m out}=S(n,d),\ &	ext{source phase algebra} \Longrightarrow 	ext{additive and inclusion--exclusion indices},\ &	ext{tensor contact} \Longrightarrow 16	ext{ and }72,\ &	ext{depth-ten exterior algebra} \Longrightarrow \mathbf{16}	ext{ with one-family gauge representations},\ &	ext{rank-}3+2	ext{ split + determinant-one fibre condition} \Longrightarrow S(U(3)	imes U(2)). \end{aligned} } The exact algebra substantially narrows the remaining physics. The open task includes deriving the source-frequency law and specifying a topologically admissible global gauge construction before deriving its projected dynamics:  oxed{ \Omega_{n,d}=S(n,d)-1 } for the occupied contact source, and  oxed{ \operatorname{Hol}(
abla^{m gauge}) \subseteq S(U(3)	imes U(2)) } for a specified determinant-trivial gauge bundle. This is not a permissible condition on the full T^{1,0}\mathbb{CP}^5 connection. Section 15 records the obstruction and the unresolved distinction between gauge and geometry. Sources and companion modules Primary companion modules: A covariant self-contact mass operator The mass spectrum selects particle depth Particle depth as spatial geometry Coupling as higher-dimensional polarization Bound within, free without The six-dimensional electron sector IDWT_02_Mass.md IDWT_03_Forces.md IDWT_07_Mode_Selection.md IDWT_09_Spectral.md Primary mathematical cross-checks: Ivan Todorov, Clifford Algebras and Spinors — Clifford factorization, spinor dimensions, and low-dimensional spin-group identities. Idrish Huet and Julieta Medina, A projective Dirac operator on \mathbb{CP}^n and extended SUSY — Dirac and Spin^c structures on complex projective spaces. Nathan Berkovits and Sergey A. Cherkis, Higher-Dimensional Twistor Transforms using Pure Spinors — projective pure-spinor geometry and the spaces SO(2n)/U(n).

