u=5,\quad d_{e\mu}=6,\quad d_	au=10. } Each assigned depth is the unique minimum even when every candidate depth receives its own optimized common scale. The observed spectrum reads back the proposed particle depths from the exponent of the combinatorial growth law. The scale-free result is: Given the independently specified mode indices and one shared scale per sector, the observed mass hierarchy selects exactly the proposed particle depths, with large residual gaps to the neighbouring integer depths. 2. Why the count depends so strongly on depth S(n,d) is a theorem of combinatorics. It has several equivalent forms: S(n,d) =\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} inom{k+d-1}{d-1} =\#\left\{(a_0,\ldots,a_d)\in\mathbb N^{d+1}: \sum_{r=0}^{d}a_r=n-1ight\}. Thus it is simultaneously the cumulative degeneracy through level n-1 of d independent bosonic oscillator directions; the number of lattice points in the standard d-simplex dilated by n-1; the dimension of the degree-(n-1) symmetric power of a (d+1)-component vector space. The hockey-stick and Pascal identities are exact: S(n,d)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}S(k+1,d-1), S(n,d)=S(n,d-1)+S(n-1,d). At large n, S(n,d)= rac{n^d}{d!}\left[1+O\!\left( rac1night)ight]. Changing d changes the shape and polynomial degree of a family spectrum. A common \mu_d changes its vertical scale. Widely separated mode indices are especially powerful depth discriminators. 3. Scale-free family tests The benchmark masses below are the particle-property values used for the scan. Quark-mass scheme dependence broadens their quantitative uncertainty; their depth minima remain unchanged. 3.1 Electroweak bosons select d=2 With (n_W,n_Z,n_H)=(76,81,95), the two independent ratios are  rac{m_Z}{m_W}= rac{S(81,d)}{S(76,d)}, \qquad  rac{m_H}{m_W}= rac{S(95,d)}{S(76,d)}. At d=2 their fractional errors are approximately +0.03\% and +0.04\%. At d=1 they become -6.07\% and -19.76\%; at d=3 they become +6.45\% and +24.41\%. These ratios are scale invariant. 3.2 Down-type quarks select d=3 The occupied counts are S(1,d),\qquad S(4,d),\qquad B_d=\sqrt{S(16,d)S(17,d)}. The third expression is the adjacent-level beat of levels 16 and 17. Relative to the down-quark mass, d=3 predicts the strange and bottom ratios to about +0.53\% and -0.09\%. At d=2 the errors are -49.73\% and -83.79\%; at d=4 they are +75.94\% and +386.91\%. The bottom mode is particularly discriminating: its geometric mean and the down and strange counts all share the same sector scale. 3.3 Up-type quarks select d=4 For (n_u,n_c,n_t)=(3,20,72), the scale-free charm/up and top/up ratios at d=4 differ from the benchmark ratios by about +0.17\% and +1.42\%. The corresponding errors at d=3 are -73.87\% and -91.89\%; at d=5 they are +243.43\% and +1001.16\%. The bare ratios isolate d=4 before interaction corrections are introduced. 3.4 Neutrino splittings select d=5 The mass-squared splitting ratio is independent of the absolute neutrino-mass scale. For (n_{
u_1},n_{
u_2},n_{
u_3})=(10,15,22), the unknown scale cancels from the normal-ordering splitting ratio: R_
u(d)=  rac{S(15,d)^2-S(10,d)^2} {S(22,d)^2-S(10,d)^2}. The atmospheric splitting is conventionally quoted as \Delta m^2_{32}. For this ratio it must first be converted to \Delta m^2_{31}=\Delta m^2_{32}+\Delta m^2_{21}. The PDG 2024 central values therefore give R_
u^{m obs}\simeq0.02976. The predictions are R_
u(4)=0.05550, \qquad R_
u(5)=0.03035, \qquad R_
u(6)=0.01686. Depth five misses this benchmark ratio by about 1.98\%. The neutrino module uses \Delta m^2_{21}=7.49	imes10^{-5} and \Delta m^2_{31}=2.534	imes10^{-3} eV^2, giving about 2.68\% instead. These are different input benchmarks, not two errors for identical inputs. Neither percentage is an uncertainty-weighted exclusion. The adjacent depths miss the benchmark used here by about 86.49\% and 43.33\%, respectively. This is a genuinely scale-free selection of the odd-dimensional neutrino sector. 3.5 Electron and muon select d=6 With (n_e,n_\mu)=(13,35), the prediction is simply  rac{m_\mu}{m_e}= rac{S(35,d)}{S(13,d)}. At d=6, S(35,6)/S(13,6)=3515/17=206.764705882\ldots. Using m_e=0.51099895 MeV and m_\mu=105.6583755 MeV gives a signed relative error of approximately -0.00173\% (predicted minus observed, divided by observed). At d=5 it is -55.00\% and at d=7 it is +115.79\%. Because the electron fixes the one common scale, each candidate depth makes a fixed muon-mass prediction. 3.6 The tau selects d=10 The tau sector shares the charged-lepton scale. Anchoring that scale with the electron gives the depth scan m_	au(d)=m_e rac{S(23,d)}{S(13,6)}. The neighbouring results are m_	au(9)=554.93\ {m MeV}, m_	au(10)=1775.79\ {m MeV}, m_	au(11)=5327.36\ {m MeV}, compared with 1776.93\ {m MeV}. The bare d=10 result is low by 0.064\%. The scale was fixed in the six-dimensional charged-lepton sector, so the tau mass tests the ten-dimensional count. 4. Scale-optimized depth scan For a family with benchmark masses M_i and counts C_i(d), allow every candidate depth to choose the common scale that minimizes logarithmic error: \log\widehat\mu_d = rac1N\sum_{i=1}^{N}\log rac{M_i}{C_i(d)}, \epsilon_d =\sqrt{ rac1N\sum_{i=1}^{N} \left[\log rac{\widehat\mu_dC_i(d)}{M_i}ight]^2}. Scanning 1\leq d\leq15 with an independently optimized scale at every depth gives: Family Selected depth RMS log error Runner-up depth RMS log error W,Z,H 2 1.77	imes10^{-4} 3 9.18	imes10^{-2} down, strange, bottom 3 2.75	imes10^{-3} 4 6.55	imes10^{-1} up, charm, top 4 6.30	imes10^{-3} 5 9.80	imes10^{-1} electron, muon 6 8.65	imes10^{-6} 7 3.85	imes10^{-1} For these four tabulated families, the runner-up depths have residuals larger by factors of roughly 155 to 44{,}500. The neutrino and tau tests use scale-free ratios and independently select d=5 and d=10. 5. Result, inputs, and operator completion Established mathematics S(n,d) is an exact simplex and cumulative-oscillator count. Its dependence on d survives optimization of a shared sector scale. At the stated indices, six separate mass hierarchies recover the six proposed depths. These are the same depth labels whose minimal projection spaces generate the candidate coupling stabilizers and spinor branchings. The mass data therefore test labels that also organize the coupling geometry. The depth fitting each family ratio is also the depth whose projection geometry supplies its candidate coupling structure. This is a cross-consistency condition between two independently computed uses of d. The dimension d is the rank of T^*Q_d in the cumulative mode bundle. Its simultaneous occurrence in the kinetic principal symbol, mass growth, and contact projection is analyzed in particle depth as spatial geometry. Conditional inputs The result is conditional on the mode indices. If both n and d were allowed to vary independently for every particle after inspecting its mass, the search would acquire enough discrete freedom to generate accidental matches. The depth test has evidential force to the extent that the mode indices are fixed by one common, mass-independent selection construction. The covariant self-contact construction derives m=\mu_dS once the projected master interaction is local on the occupied coherent mode ray. One shared \mu_d further requires that its scalar contact strength be independent of the band label N=n-1. The family ratios provide evidence for these two operator properties; their final proof belongs to the master-kernel projection. The remaining geometry problem The simplex count is the exact local cumulative count for d independent quadratic directions. For the electron sector Q_6=\mathbb{CP}^3, the holomorphic magnetic-section count is \dim H^0(\mathbb{CP}^3,\mathcal O(L))= inom{L+3}{3} while the cumulative mass-mode count is S(n,6)= inom{n+5}{6}. The first counts degree-L magnetic sections; the second is the rank of the cumulative mode bundle \mathcal E_{n-1,d} =\operatorname{Sym}^{n-1} \left(\mathbf1\oplus T^*Q_d\otimes\mathbb Cight), \qquad \operatorname{rank}\mathcal E_{n-1,d}=S(n,d). The normalized coherent localization density on its projective ray fibre has one nonzero eigenvalue S(n,d). Thus \mathcal M_{n,d}([z]) =\mu_dS(n,d)|z;n-1angle\langle z;n-1| derives the mass law on the localized eigenline. The complete construction and its hypotheses are given in the covariant self-contact mass operator. The remaining geometry problem is to obtain this contact endomorphism by projecting the master self-kernel. 6. Falsifiable next calculation The decisive next step is to calculate P_{d,N}\mathcal K_\infty P_{d,N} and prove that its scalar contact channel obeys P_{d,N}\mathcal K_\infty P_{d,N} =\mu_dS(N+1,d)|z;Nangle\langle z;N|. The projected calculation must supply one shared \mu_d for all occupied modes of sector d; a self-interaction local on the occupied coherent mode ray; a selection rule for the stated n values; how the odd-depth sphere sectors and even-depth projective sectors realize the same simplex law; the class of interaction corrections that preserves the bare depth test. The rank, coherent normalization, and mass eigenvalue then follow as theorems. If the projection produces an identity, kinetic, or ray-averaged quartic operator instead, the numerical recovery remains real but the proposed self-contact origin is false. 7. Data reference The benchmark values are drawn from the Particle Data Group summary tables; the scan should be rerun whenever the reference inputs or quark-mass schemes are changed: Particle Data Group, Review of Particle Physics 2024. The complete scan and its assertions are executable in the V2 verification program.

