Physical premise (IDWT): There is one infinite-dimensional physical manifold. All sectors form one nested chain of submanifolds within it. Particle depth counts ordinary, literal macroscopic spatial dimensions. “Observed” and “unobserved” describe an observer's access to those dimensions, not different kinds of physical dimension. See spatial meaning and mathematical scope. 1. Result The present V2 weak-transition operator is P_3D_\infty P_4 \supset g_2\gamma^\mu W_\mu^-T^-P_{(1,1)}\otimes\mathcal C_{34}, with the general cross-sector factorization P_dD_\infty P_e = \mathcal I_{de}^{m pol}\otimes\mathcal C_{de}^{m mode} +\mathcal R_{de}. The existing construction fixes the weak polarization intertwiner and the occupied quark modes, but it does not yet define a nontrivial operator on the three-dimensional generation space. Under the additional assumption that the leading band-blind weak vertex identifies aligned up- and down-type mass eigenbases, the interaction is generation diagonal. This aligned benchmark gives  oxed{V_{m CKM}^{(0)}=I_3.} This is not the observed CKM matrix. A generation-blind vertex in a common interaction basis does not by itself imply this result in the mass basis: in the usual W^+ convention, independent left mass diagonalizations give V=U_{u,L}^\dagger U_{d,L}. The eigenvalue counts do not fix those eigenvectors or their relative orientation. Thus V2 has not yet predicted either I_3 or the observed nontrivial CKM matrix without a further basis/vertex assumption. The CKM problem isolates a missing piece of the projected master operator:  oxed{ 	ext{V2 requires a nontrivial generation-space component of } P_3D_\infty P_4. } That component must be derived from the cross-sector coherent contact, the remainder \mathcal R_{34}, or both. It cannot be inserted as an arbitrary unitary matrix without destroying the predictive purpose of the construction. 2. The occupied quark bases The committed up-type occupied modes are (n_u,n_c,n_t)=(3,20,72), at depth d_u=4, while the down-type sector contains n_d=1, \qquad n_s=4, and the bottom state is the critical adjacent-band pair (n_{b,-},n_{b,+})=(16,17). Its mass is m_b =\mu_3\sqrt{S(16,3)S(17,3)}. Thus the physical three-generation spaces may be written schematically as \mathcal F_u =\operatorname{span}\{|uangle,|cangle,|tangle\}, \mathcal F_d =\operatorname{span}\{|dangle,|sangle,|bangle\}. The first two down states are ordinary occupied coherent bands. The bottom state is already exceptional because it is represented by a two-band singular-value construction. Any CKM derivation must respect that distinction rather than replacing the bottom state by an arbitrary ordinary mode. 3. Where CKM lives in the projected operator Restrict the cross-sector weak block to the physical generation spaces: C_{34} = P_{\mathcal F_d}\mathcal C_{34}P_{\mathcal F_u}. In chosen orthonormal mass bases, (C_{34})_{ij} = \langle d_i|\mathcal C_{34}|u_jangle, where (d_1,d_2,d_3)=(d,s,b), (u_1,u_2,u_3)=(u,c,t). The charged-current interaction then has the form \mathcal L_W \supset  rac{g_2}{\sqrt2} \overline{d_{Li}}\gamma^\mu (C_{34})_{ij}u_{Lj}W^-_\mu +	ext{h.c.} Canonical normalization is determined by the kinetic metrics, not by an arbitrary polar or singular-value decomposition of the vertex. For a transition matrix K before canonical normalization and positive kinetic metrics Z_u,Z_d, define K_c=Z_d^{-1/2}KZ_u^{-1/2},\qquad V_-=L_d^\dagger K_cL_u, where L_u,L_d diagonalize the canonically normalized left-handed mass operators. For already orthonormal mass bases, these transformations are identities. The three-generation charged current is unitary only if K_c^\dagger K_c=I \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad K^\dagger Z_d^{-1}K=Z_u. This is an additional property to derive, not a permission to discard the singular values of K. A deficient-rank kernel cannot be repaired by invertible kinetic normalization; its polar unitary completion is nonunique. The rows here are (d,s,b) and columns (u,c,t), so V_- is the adjoint of the usual up-row/down-column CKM matrix: V_{m CKM}=V_-^\dagger. Below, identity and zero-mixing conclusions are independent of this convention. See the normalization audit. 4. Leading-order no-go theorem Assume the following particular leading ansatz. Generation preservation is an extra interaction assumption, not a consequence of the diagonal mass law: each occupied quark mass eigenstate is localized on its own coherent mode line; the scalar contact coefficient is band blind; the weak polarization intertwiner \mathcal I_{34}^{m pol} acts only on the weak/color polarization factors; the leading mode contact does not mix distinct occupied generation bands; no generation-dependent contribution from \mathcal R_{34} is retained; the kinetic-normalized charged current satisfies the isometry condition in Section 3. Then on the physical generation space \mathcal C_{34} \propto \sum_{i=1}^3 |d_iangle\langle u_i|, up to the kinetic metrics and removable phases. Physical generation-dependent vertex strengths cannot simply be declared state normalizations. After canonical normalization, C_{34}\longrightarrow I_3, hence  oxed{V_{m CKM}^{(0)}=I_3.} The Jarlskog invariant is then  oxed{J^{(0)}=0.} This conditional diagonal, isometric ansatz cannot reproduce inter-generation transitions or CKM CP violation. Without the isometry assumption, it gives a diagonal vertex, not necessarily I_3; diagonality still supplies no mixing. Band blindness alone proves neither diagonality nor isometry. 5. Why the mass hierarchy does not determine CKM The simplex mass laws determine diagonal mass eigenvalues: M_u =\mu_4\operatorname{diag} \left( S(3,4), S(20,4), S(72,4) ight), while the down sector has the schematic form M_d =\mu_3\operatorname{diag} \left( S(1,3), S(4,3), \sqrt{S(16,3)S(17,3)} ight). These spectra fix eigenvalues but not the relative orientation of the up and down eigenbases. For any unitary matrices U_u and U_d, M_u \mapsto U_uM_uU_u^\dagger, M_d \mapsto U_dM_dU_d^\dagger preserves the eigenvalue spectra. The charged-current mixing matrix is V_{m CKM}=U_u^\dagger U_d. This is the usual up-row/down-column W^+ convention. The down-row/up-column matrix V_- used for the preceding cross-sector kernel is its adjoint, V_-=U_d^\dagger U_u; the two must not be given the same matrix label. Therefore the successful simplex mass hierarchy alone cannot determine CKM. A separate geometric object fixing the relative orientation of the two three-dimensional flavor frames is mathematically necessary. Any proposal that constructs CKM solely from the six quark masses, with no independently derived cross-sector operator, adds an extra ansatz rather than following from the present V2 mass theorem. 6. The minimal V2 completion The minimal completion is a generation-space operator G_{34}:\mathcal F_u	o\mathcal F_d appearing in P_3D_\infty P_4 \supset g_2\gamma^\mu W_\mu^-T^-P_{(1,1)} \otimes G_{34}. Its matrix elements must be obtained from the same projected master geometry: (G_{34})_{ij} = \langle d_i| P_3D_\infty P_4 |u_jangle_{m normalized}. A successful derivation must produce all of the following without fitting CKM data: a dominant 1\leftrightarrow1, 2\leftrightarrow2, 3\leftrightarrow3 structure; a much larger 1\leftrightarrow2 transition than 2\leftrightarrow3; a strongly suppressed 1\leftrightarrow3 transition; one irreducible complex phase; unitarity after canonical normalization; the same universal weak coupling g_2 for all generations. In schematic magnitude form the target hierarchy is |V_{us}|\sim2	imes10^{-1}, \qquad |V_{cb}|\sim4	imes10^{-2}, \qquad |V_{ub}|\sim4	imes10^{-3}. The hierarchy between these three off-diagonal amplitudes is itself a stringent geometric test. 7. Candidate geometric sources already present in V2 The current V2 construction contains several structures capable in principle of generating G_{34}, but none has yet been evaluated as a three-generation matrix. 7.1 Cross-sector coherent contact The general factorization already contains \mathcal C_{34}^{m mode}. If its normalized matrix elements between distinct occupied coherent bands are nonzero, \langle d_i|\mathcal C_{34}^{m mode}|u_jangle
eq0, \qquad i
eq j, then CKM mixing can arise directly from the pushed-forward contact kernel. The present files specify only its role in source phase and mode counting, not these off-diagonal matrix elements. 7.2 Extrinsic and derivative remainder The cross-sector operator contains \mathcal R_{34}, which collects extrinsic, derivative, and nonlocal corrections. A generation-dependent normal connection or second-fundamental-form term could rotate the quark mass frames: G_{34} = G_{34}^{(0)} +\delta G_{34}^{m extrinsic}. If the leading term is diagonal, the observed small CKM angles make this a mathematically natural perturbative possibility. It is not yet a derivation. 7.3 Holonomy of a generation bundle If the three occupied up modes and three occupied down modes define rank-three bundles over a shared observer or contact base, a relative transport operator could be U_{34}(\gamma) = \mathcal P\exp \left( -\int_\gamma A_{34} ight). Then V_{m CKM} = U_d^\dagger U_u would be a genuine relative holonomy. The CP phase would be an invariant phase of this transport, not an inserted parameter. However, the present V2 documents define normal holonomy for gauge reduction, not a rank-three generation connection A_{34}. This is therefore a concrete missing construction. 7.4 Bottom two-band structure The bottom state already involves an off-diagonal singular-value problem between the adjacent bands 16 and 17. This proves that V2 is not restricted in principle to purely diagonal band operators. The CKM calculation should therefore examine whether the same projected master kernel that produces the bottom singular value also generates weak transition matrix elements between the occupied up- and down-sector bands. This is the most economical place to search for nontrivial generation mixing because it uses an off-diagonal mechanism already required elsewhere in V2. 8. A finite matrix problem The CKM calculation can be reduced to a finite operator computation. Let the up-sector normalized occupied states be |Uangle = \left( |3,4angle, |20,4angle, |72,4angle ight), and let the down-sector states be |Dangle = \left( |1,3angle, |4,3angle, |b,3angle ight), where |b,3angle is the normalized singular vector of the 16--17 bottom block. Define K_{ij} = \langle D_i| P_3D_\infty P_4 |U_jangle. Remove the derived universal polarization and weak-coupling factors and use the independently derived kinetic metrics and mass-basis rotations:  oxed{ V_{m CKM}^{m V2} =\left(L_d^\dagger Z_d^{-1/2}KZ_u^{-1/2}L_uight)^\dagger. } This is a standard unitary CKM candidate only after the isometry test in Section 3 passes. Taking an arbitrary polar factor is not this calculation. No quark mass values are required to choose its mixing angles once the occupied mode projectors and master kernel are fixed. This 3	imes3 calculation is the decisive CKM test. 9. Falsification criteria The CKM sector falsifies or constrains V2 in several clean ways. Failure A: exact band diagonality If the kinetic-normalized current in the physical mass bases is diagonal, K_{ij}=0 \qquad(i
eq j), it gives no generation mixing. If it also satisfies the isometry condition, its removable diagonal phases give V_{m CKM}=I_3, which is experimentally false. A diagonal nonisometric vertex is not a unitary CKM matrix at all. Failure B: real transition matrix only If every element of the normalized mass-basis charged-current matrix can be made simultaneously real by rephasing the quark states, then J=0, and V2 fails to reproduce observed quark CP violation. Failure C: generic order-one mixing If the normalized off-diagonal elements are generically of the same order as the diagonal ones, the observed hierarchical CKM structure is not reproduced. Failure D: fitted generation rotation If an arbitrary U(3) rotation must be introduced after the projected operator is calculated, then CKM has not been derived. The model has merely reintroduced the Standard Model mixing parameters. Success criterion A successful V2 derivation must calculate a unique or discretely selected K from the master projection and recover both the approximate CKM hierarchy and a nonzero rephasing-invariant CP phase. 10. Status The present CKM calculation therefore has a definite outcome:  oxed{ 	ext{stated generation-diagonal, isometric leading ansatz} \Longrightarrow V_{m CKM}=I_3, } which is not physical. This does not falsify the full projected-master framework because the existing V2 documents explicitly leave the cross-sector projector and remainder \mathcal R_{34} uncalculated. It does falsify the stronger claim that the already-written leading band-blind weak contact is sufficient to explain quark mixing. The problem has now been reduced to one explicit missing object:  oxed{ K_{ij} = \langle D_i|P_3D_\infty P_4|U_jangle. } Computing this finite matrix from the master kernel is the next CKM calculation. The comparison must use the kinetic-normalized mass-basis current and verify its unitarity, mixing magnitudes, and rephasing-invariant CP violation without fitting CKM data. Reality of 